Menorrhagia (Heavy Menstrual Bleeding)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Menstrual flow >7 days, Soaking multiple pads per hour, Passage of large clots

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-023
Reason for Visit: Acute, massive vaginal bleeding with passage of large clots causing severe presyncope and generalized weakness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and sister
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute hemodynamic compromise (severe dizziness on standing, pallor) secondary to extremely heavy, uncontrolled menstrual bleeding (soaking multiple pads per hour). Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess for hypovolemic shock, initiate immediate pharmacological hemostasis, and determine the need for emergency ambulance transport before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am bleeding through a super pad and a tampon every 30 minutes, passing huge clots, and I feel like I am going to pass out if I stand up.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a six-month history of increasingly heavy menses lasting longer than 7 days, restricting her daily activities. Over the last 12 hours, the bleeding has become catastrophic, soaking through multiple sanitary products per hour and necessitating the use of double protection. She is passing large blood clots (golf-ball sized). She complains of profound symptoms of chronic anemia (fatigue, lethargy, shortness of breath on exertion) that have acutely worsened today with the heavy blood loss. She reports waking up continuously during the night to change sanitary protection.
Functional Status: Confined to a supine position on the bed; unable to sit up without severe dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of hypovolemic shock; sister is present and highly concerned.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient exhibits marked pallor of the skin and conjunctivae, is diaphoretic, and appears acutely lethargic.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, with mild lower pelvic pressure but no acute peritonism or severe guarding. Speculum examination (performed carefully in bed) reveals a large volume of clotted blood in the vaginal vault; active, brisk, dark red bleeding is observed coming from the cervical os. No vaginal or cervical lacerations. Bimanual exam reveals an enlarged, irregularly shaped uterus (suggestive of leiomyomas) palpable approximately to the umbilicus.
Environmental Observations: Heavily blood-soaked towels and clothing present; environment indicates massive blood loss.
Medication Reconciliation: No active medications. Not taking iron supplements.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute severe Menorrhagia (likely secondary to uterine leiomyomas) with symptomatic acute-on-chronic blood loss anemia - Status: Hemodynamically borderline
Differentials:

Miscarriage / Incomplete Abortion - Ruled out by a negative point-of-care urine beta-hCG test.

Bleeding Diathesis (e.g., von Willebrand disease) - Unlikely as the primary cause given the palpable, enlarged, irregular uterus strongly suggesting a structural fibroid etiology, though a secondary coagulopathy from massive loss is possible.

Endometrial Carcinoma - A differential for heavy bleeding, but the irregularly enlarged uterus is more classically consistent with fibroids; requires formal imaging later.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Impending hypovolemic shock)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with acute, life-threatening menstrual hemorrhage resulting in objective signs of hypovolemia (tachycardia, hypotension, marked conjunctival pallor) and symptomatic presyncope. Direct visualization confirms massive, active uterine bleeding and an enlarged, irregular uterus. The sheer volume of blood loss (soaking >1 pad/hour) and the resultant hemodynamic instability mandated immediate after-hours intervention to administer high-dose tranexamic acid. Due to the borderline blood pressure and resting tachycardia, immediate ambulance transfer to a hospital with gynecological and blood transfusion capabilities was required to safely stabilize the patient, an intervention that absolutely could not wait for in-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral tranexamic acid 1g immediately to initiate clot stabilization. Placed the patient in a strict supine position with legs elevated to maximize cerebral perfusion. Activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for priority transport to the local hospital Emergency Department for intravenous fluid resuscitation, likely blood transfusion, and definitive gynecological management (e.g., IV estrogen or surgical intervention).
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain adequate blood pressure to your brain, remain completely flat on the bed and do not attempt to stand or walk under any circumstances; recognizing the immediate reduction in dizziness will enforce strict compliance. To assist the medical team, keep all used pads and towels in a plastic bag; understanding this provides an accurate visual estimation of blood loss will ensure cooperation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Emergency Medical Services (Ambulance) activated and on route.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Direct clinical handover provided to the responding paramedic crew.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched for immediate emergency transport to the hospital due to high risk of hypovolemic shock.

